One design change makes this substantially better though.

Don't measure a binary. "Can an agent use the web, yes or no" gives you one number and a shrug. Measure the gradient: run the same task on the same site under different conditions and see where the line moves.

Vanilla headless browser. Stealth on. Stealth plus a residential IP in the site's own country. Same task, same site, three configs. Now you can say something much sharper than "the web is closed" — you can say exactly how much effort it takes to look human, and which categories of site can't be beaten at all. That's a chart with three lines going in different directions, which is a better artifact than one bar.

It also happens to exercise the exact features Solari sells, which the people reading this built. They'll notice.

What you're actually classifying

The hard part isn't driving the browser. It's deciding what happened, and this is where the project either works or turns to mush. You need a taxonomy before you write any code:

Got through cleanly. Got through after a captcha. Hard blocked with a 403 or a challenge wall. Soft blocked, where the page loads but the content is degraded or empty. Shadow blocked, where everything looks fine but the action silently doesn't take. Site was just down.

Soft and shadow blocking are the ones that will eat your weekend. A page that returns 200 with nothing useful on it looks identical to success unless you're checking for the thing you expected to find. Decide upfront how you detect each state, and use a model to judge screenshots rather than trying to write rules per site.

Where it's harder than you think

IP reputation dominates everything. If you run all of it from one datacenter IP, you're not measuring the web, you're measuring that IP's reputation degrading over the course of the run. Rotate, and record which IP did what.

Flake will be constant. Same site, same config, different result on two runs. Run each cell three times minimum or your numbers are noise, and that triples your cost, which you should price before you start rather than after.

One line to stay on

Keep it to reading and reaching, not mass account creation. Loading pages, attempting a search, trying to reach a product or listing — that's measurement, and it's defensible. Creating five hundred fake accounts on real services is a ToS problem you'd have to defend in the replies, and it would step on the finding. Same data either way. Much better post.

A scope that actually finishes

Fifty sites, not five hundred, across five categories. One task each. Three configs. Three runs. That's 450 sessions, which is enough for real numbers and small enough to debug. If it works and you have time left, scale the site list, since that part is just a loop.

Want me to draft the task suite and the classification taxonomy? That's the piece worth getting right before any code, and it's about an hour of thinking that saves you a day.

