What it is

A site checker. Someone gives it a URL. It tries to use that site the way an AI assistant would, then tells them whether that works and where it broke.

That's it. Like a speed test, but for whether software acting on a person's behalf can get through your front door.

Why anyone cares

Companies are building agents to go do things on the web. Websites are simultaneously getting better at blocking anything that isn't a human. Nobody knows how bad the collision is, because nobody has actually gone and checked.

How it works, step by step

Someone pastes in a URL. You spin up a cloud browser and point it at the page. The browser tries to do one basic thing — search for something, open a listing, reach a product page. You take screenshots the whole way through.

Then a model looks at those screenshots and answers one question: did this work, or did something stop us? If something stopped it, which thing — a captcha, a block page, a login wall, or a page that loaded but came back empty.

Then you do it again, twice more, with a better disguise. First run is a plain browser, obviously automated. Second turns on stealth so it looks like a normal Chrome. Third adds an IP address from a real home connection in that country. Same site, same task, three levels of effort.

The result you hand back is: an agent gets in at this level of disguise, and not below it. Plus the recording, so they can watch the moment it got stopped.

Then the part that makes the post

You run it yourself across fifty sites you didn't pick at random — news, shopping, banks, government, booking. Same task shape on all of them. You publish which categories are already sealed shut and how much effort the rest takes.

Two deliverables from one build: a tool people can run on their own site, and a number nobody has.

What you're actually writing

A loop that opens a browser and clicks a few things. A prompt that reads screenshots and picks one of six labels. A small web page with a text box and a live view. A script that runs the whole list and dumps a spreadsheet.

Not much. The hard part isn't any of those, it's deciding correctly whether a page that loaded but looks wrong counts as blocked. That's the actual engineering, and it's mostly prompt work plus staring at screenshots.

